{
  "slug": "random-pick-with-weight",
  "title": "Random Pick with Weight",
  "topic": "binary-search",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "Prefix Sum / binary search lower bound",
  "statement": "A loaded selector is described by a list of positive weights. Picture a line segment split into bins: bin 0 owns the first `weights[0]` units of length, bin 1 owns the next `weights[1]` units, and so on, so the whole segment has length equal to the sum of all weights.\n\nA draw is an integer `r`. The selector returns the index of the bin that contains position `r`, which is the smallest index `i` with `weights[0] + ... + weights[i] > r`. A draw below 0 is treated as 0, and a draw at or above the total weight is treated as `total - 1`, so every draw yields a valid index even when the draw is outside the segment.\n\nThe array `picks` holds one draw per query. Return one selected index per draw, in the same order, as an array of the same length.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= weights.length <= 100` and `1 <= weights[i] <= 10000` and `1 <= picks.length <= 100` and `0 <= picks[j] <= 100000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "weights = [1], picks = [0]",
      "output": "[0]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "weights = [7712, 3645], picks = [17, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 68]",
      "output": "[0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(weights: number[], picks: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Turn the weights into running sums first: a draw r belongs to the first bin whose running sum is strictly greater than r.",
    "Every weight is positive, so the running sums are strictly increasing, which means you can binary search for that first larger entry instead of scanning.",
    "Clamp each draw into [0, total - 1] before searching, then return the index of the first prefix sum greater than the clamped value."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "Because the weights are positive, the running sums (prefix sums) of `weights` are strictly increasing, and bin i covers exactly the half-open range from prefix[i - 1] to prefix[i] - 1. Each query therefore reduces to one question: which is the first running sum that is strictly greater than the clamped draw? The brute force answers it by re-accumulating from the left for every draw, which is O(n) per query and O(n * m) overall for m draws. The optimal solution pays that O(n) scan once to build the prefix array, then binary searches each draw for the first entry greater than r; the search is the standard lower-bound loop that keeps the invariant that every candidate index before lo has a running sum that is too small, so the range halves every step and the answer is found in at most log2(n) comparisons.",
    "why_optimal": "The prefix array costs O(n) to build and the per-query search is a comparison search over n sorted values, so O(m log n) is optimal up to constants: with n <= 100 that is at most 7 comparisons per draw against up to 100 for the scan, and with m <= 100 the whole run is a few thousand steps. A comparison-based search cannot beat O(log n) per query because the answer is one of n positions and each comparison only tells the search which side to keep, so this matches the per-query lower bound; the only way to do better is to trade memory for O(1) queries with a bucket table, which costs O(total weight) space and is far worse here since total weight can reach 10^6.",
    "pitfalls": "Testing prefix[i] >= r instead of prefix[i] > r shifts every draw one bin to the left, because bin i owns the half-open range that starts at prefix[i - 1]. Forgetting to clamp draws at or above the total weight makes the binary search run off the end of the array instead of returning the last bin, and forgetting to clamp negative draws does the same at the other end; both implementations must clamp identically or they disagree on the boundary draws. Recomputing the prefix sums inside the query loop turns the optimal solution back into the brute force. In the binary search itself, using hi = mid - 1 with the strict greater-than test skips the only index that can satisfy the predicate, and a midpoint computed as (lo + hi) / 2 without flooring stalls the loop."
  },
